---
dewey: "900"
description: Use ALWAYS when asked to CREATE A RULE or UPDATE A RULE or taught a lesson from the user that should be retained as a new rule
alwaysApply: false
globs: "**/*.mdc"
---
# LLM Rules Format

## Agent behavior

When asked to create or update a rule, or to capture a lesson learned as a
new rule:

1. Pick the right Dewey category (see `## Dewey Ranges` below) and put the
   numeric code in frontmatter (`dewey: "NNN"`), never in the filename or
   path.
2. Choose activation. Either `alwaysApply: true` (injected into every
   prompt) or `globs: [...]` (scoped to matching files). A rule with
   neither is never injected.
3. Author the body to the `## Core Structure` template. Keep it tight — see
   `## Formatting Guidelines` and `## AI Context Efficiency` for length and
   token-density expectations.
4. Add a `## Agent behavior` section yourself if the rule prescribes a
   distinct agent workflow; the loader uses it (or the first body
   paragraph) as the prompt-injected directive.
5. Update the master index (`index.mdc`) and `agents.md` in this
   repo, and the consuming repo's `CLAUDE.md` quick-reference table.

## Core Structure

```mdc
---
dewey: "NNN"
description: ACTION when TRIGGER to OUTCOME
# Activation: include at least one; both are allowed together.
alwaysApply: true           # inject into every prompt regardless of file context
# globs:
#   - "src/**/*.clj"        # scope to matching files (combine with alwaysApply if needed)
---

# Rule Title

## Context
- When to apply this rule
- Prerequisites or conditions

## Requirements
- Concise, actionable items
- Each requirement must be testable

## Examples
<example>
Good concise example with explanation
</example>

<example type="invalid">
Invalid concise example with explanation
</example>
```

## File Organization

### Location

- Root: repo root (consumed as `.standards/` git submodule in product repos)
- Extension: `.mdc`
- Structure: `<category-slug>/<rule-slug>.mdc`
- Dewey code: in frontmatter (`dewey: "NNN"`), not in the filename or path

```
<standards-root>/           # repo root (or .standards/ when used as submodule)
├── index.mdc               # Master catalog
├── foundations/            # dewey 000-099
├── languages/              # dewey 200-299
├── frameworks/             # dewey 300-399
├── testing/                # dewey 400-499
├── documentation/          # dewey 600-699
├── workflows/              # dewey 700-799
├── project/                # dewey 800-899
└── meta/                   # dewey 900-999
```

### Dewey Ranges

```
000-099  Foundations & Core Principles
100-199  Development Environment & Tools
200-299  Languages
300-399  Frameworks & Platforms
400-499  Testing & Quality
500-599  Operations & Infrastructure
600-699  Documentation
700-799  Workflows & Processes
800-899  Project-Specific
900-999  Meta & Templates
```

Private rules: `_name.mdc` (underscore prefix)

### Glob Pattern Examples

Common glob patterns for different rule types:

- All rules: `**/*.mdc`
- Language rules: src/**/*.{js,ts}
- Testing standards: **/*.test.{js,ts}
- React components: src/components/**/*.tsx
- Documentation: docs/**/*.md
- Configuration files: *.config.{js,json}
- Build artifacts: dist/**/*
- Multiple extensions: src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}
- Multiple files: dist/**/*, docs/**/*.md

## Frontmatter Fields

Every rule file opens with a YAML frontmatter block. The full set of recognised
fields is listed below. Keep the block minimal — include only fields the rule
actually needs.

### Always required

| Field | Type | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| `dewey` | string | Dewey classification number, e.g. `"210"`. Quoted. |
| `description` | string | Activation hint in ACTION TRIGGER OUTCOME format. Aim for <120 chars when feasible; never truncate to the point of losing meaning. |

### Activation — include at least one; both permitted together

| Field | Type | When to use |
|-------|------|-------------|
| `alwaysApply` | boolean | `true` — inject rule into every agent prompt regardless of file context. Use for universal principles (stratified design, result handling, etc.). `false` — explicit opt-out (rare; most rules without `alwaysApply` simply omit it). |
| `globs` | string or list | Path patterns scoping the rule to matching files. Use when the rule is language- or file-type-specific. A rule with neither `alwaysApply: true` nor `globs` is effectively never injected. |

Rules may carry both `alwaysApply: true` and `globs` (e.g. `languages/clojure.mdc`
combines universal injection with a glob that helps IDE tooling narrow context).

### Automated-fix fields (optional)

Used by lint runners and agent auto-fix pipelines. Only include when the rule
has a mechanical fix that can be applied programmatically.

| Field | Type | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| `detection.mode` | `positive` \| `negative` | `positive` — flag matches; `negative` — flag absences. |
| `detection.pattern` | string | Regex the runner applies against file content. |
| `detection.emailPattern` | string | Secondary regex (e.g. for copyright checks). |
| `enforcement.action` | `hard-halt` \| `require-approval` \| `warn` \| `audit` | How a pack-derived gate treats a violation. `hard-halt` **blocks** (fails the gate); the others are non-blocking (recorded / surfaced). Omit to default — `warn` for `alwaysApply` rules, `audit` for advisory ones. A blocking rule compiles at `:major` severity or higher. |
| `enforcement.message` | string | Override the default `"Standard: <title>"` violation message. |
| `remediation.strategy` | string | `mechanical` (auto-apply) or `manual`. |
| `remediation.replacement` | string | Replacement expression (supports `$N` capture groups). |
| `remediation.type` | string | `prepend`, `append`, `replace`, etc. |
| `remediation.template` | string | Full text to insert when `remediation.type` is `prepend`/`append`. |
| `remediation.autoFixable` | boolean | `true` if the runner may apply the fix without confirmation. |
| `remediation.excludePathContains` | list | Skip files whose path contains any of these substrings. |
| `remediation.excludeCurrentContains` | list | Skip when the matched line already contains any of these strings. |

### Discouraged / non-standard fields

`title`, `category`, `tags`, `confidence`, `impact` — occasionally appear in
older rules (e.g. `workflows/git-worktrees.mdc`). These are not processed by
any loader; avoid adding them to new rules.

### Body

- <version>X.Y.Z</version>
- context: Usage conditions
- requirements: Actionable items
- examples: Both valid and invalid

## Formatting Guidelines

- Use Concise Markdown primarily
- XML tags limited to:
  - <example>
  - <danger>
  - <required>
  - <rules>
  - <rule>
  - <critical>
  - <version>
- Always indent content within XML or nested XML tags by 2 spaces
- Keep rules as short as possible
- Use Mermaid syntax if it will be shorter or clearer than describing a complex rule
- Use Emojis where appropriate to convey meaning that will improve rule understanding by the AI Agent
- Keep examples as short as possible to clearly convey the positive or negative example

## Authoring discipline — self-consistency

A rule is normative the moment it merges; an error in it propagates to every agent that
reads it. Before opening a rule PR, verify the document against itself:

- **Example code is normative — trace it as if compiled.** Readers copy it verbatim. Apply
  the same scrutiny a real diff gets: walk the types, the nil/default paths, the return value.
  A markdown fence is not an excuse to skip review. (PR #39: an `Int` example claimed an
  `Int64` round-trip in prose but returned `n.intValue` — a doc/code drift inside the example.)
- **Every summary, "what not to do," and catalog line must agree with the detailed rule it
  condenses.** Re-read the section as a whole and check each short restatement against the body;
  a punchy summary that contradicts the rule is worse than no summary. (PR #36: a docstring rule
  said both "delete redundant docstrings" and "preserve docstrings." PR #39: a summary implied
  `as? Bool`-first was a safe type test when the body said it was ambiguous.)
- **No internal contradictions.** When a rule states a constraint in more than one place
  (frontmatter description, body, examples, cross-references), they must say the same thing.

This is the rule-authoring application of `code-review-rigor` obligations 4 ("First-run /
default-value / nil-path scrutiny") and 5 ("Intent, not symptoms"): the document is the artifact
under review.

## AI Optimization Tips

1. Use precise, deterministic ACTION TRIGGER OUTCOME format in descriptions
2. Provide concise positive and negative example of rule application in practice
3. Optimize for AI context window efficiency
4. Remove any non-essential or redundant information
5. Use standard glob patterns without quotes (e.g., *.js, src/**/*.ts)

## AI Context Efficiency

1. Aim for frontmatter description under 120 characters while maintaining clear intent for rule selection by AI Agent; never truncate to the point of losing meaning
2. Limit examples to essential patterns only
3. Use hierarchical structure for quick parsing
4. Remove redundant information across sections
5. Maintain high information density with minimal tokens
6. Focus on machine-actionable instructions over human explanations

<critical>
  - NEVER include verbose explanations or redundant context that increases AI token overhead
  - Keep file as short and to the point as possible BUT NEVER at the expense of sacrificing
    rule impact and usefulness for the AI Agent.
  - Frontmatter must contain only fields from the schema above. Do NOT invent new fields.
  - Every rule must have `dewey` and `description`. Every rule must have either `alwaysApply`
    or `globs` (or both) — a rule with neither is never injected.
  - `detection.*` and `remediation.*` fields are only appropriate when a mechanical fix exists.
</critical>
